<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=apollo-11">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apollo 11</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1969">1969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> 11 (16 to 24 July 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon, the fifth crewed mission of NASA's Apollo program. Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin landed in the lunar module Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on 20 July at 20:17 UTC while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-collins">Michael Collins</a> stayed in lunar orbit in the command module Columbia. They spent around two and a half hours walking on the surface and collected 21.5 kg of lunar material. The mission met the goal President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a> set in May 1961, and the moonwalk was broadcast to an estimated 600 million viewers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apollo-11.jpg" alt="Apollo 11" width="330" height="334" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Neil A. Armstrong · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Man_on_the_Moon,_AS11-40-5903_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A modest engineer, he largely avoided the celebrity that followed <b>Apollo 11</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-25">25 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Apollo 11</b> met the goal in July 1969.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-25">25 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Apollo 11</b> landed seven years later.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-12">12 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
